- *Non-leap Feb?* Add a `rand bit leap` and make `day <= (leap ? 29 : 28)`.
- *Power of 2 only?* Drop the `32'hAAAA_AAAA` mask.

hwinterview-044:
**Key idea:** These are four separate patterns, each built from one standard tool:
- **Weighted bit count:** choose `count` with `dist`, solve it first, then `$countones(word) == count`.
- **Single run of ones:** pick `length` and `start`, then `word == mask << start`.
- **No adjacent ones:** `(word & (word << 1)) == 0`.
- **Balanced:** `$countones(word) == WIDTH/2`.

| Pattern | Example (16-bit view) |
| --- | --- |
| weighted count = 3 | `0100_0000_1000_0010` |
| one run (len 5, start 4) | `0000_0001_1111_0000` |
| no adjacent ones | `1010_0100_1001_0101` |
| balanced (8 of 16) | `1100_1010_0110_0011` |

### Solution
```systemverilog
class weighted_count;
  rand bit [9:0] word;
  rand int       count;
  constraint c {
    count dist {0 :/ 5, [1:3] :/ 50, [4:10] :/ 45};   // range total weights
    solve count before word;
    $countones(word) == count;
  }
endclass

class one_run;
  rand bit [31:0]   word;
  rand int unsigned start, length;
  constraint c {
    length inside {[1:32]};
    start  inside {[0:31]};
    start + length <= 32;
    word == ((32'hFFFF_FFFF >> (32 - length)) << start);
    solve length before word; solve start before word;
  }
endclass

class separated_ones;
  rand bit [31:0] word;
  constraint c { (word & (word << 1)) == 0; }
endclass

class balanced_bits;
  rand bit [31:0] word;
  constraint c { $countones(word) == 16; }
endclass

// Exactly 10 non-adjacent ones without $countones
class ten_separated;
  rand bit b[32];
  constraint c {
    b.sum() with (int'(item)) == 10;
    foreach (b[i]) if (i > 0) !(b[i] && b[i-1]);
  }
endclass
```

### Explanation
- `:=` gives **each value** in a range that weight. `:/` **splits** the weight across the range. `[4:10] :/ 45` means 45 total, about 6.4 each.
- Without `solve count before word`, the solver tends to favor counts near 5 (C(10,5) = 252 words against C(10,1) = 10). The `dist` still applies, but making the order explicit is cleaner.
- "Lower two bits equal only 5% of the time": `rand bit eq; eq dist {1 := 5, 0 := 95}; (word[1] == word[0]) == eq;`.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `word << 1` in `separated_ones` drops bit 31. That's fine, since bit 31 has no left neighbor.
- `32'hFFFF_FFFF >> 32` is 0 in SV (shift amount >= width), so `length >= 1` is needed.

### Follow-ups
- *Two runs of ones separated by at least one zero?* Two `(start, length)` pairs with `start2 > start1 + len1`.
- *Runs only at even offsets?* `start % 2 == 0`.

hwinterview-045:
**Key idea:** Separate *cycle-based* history from *instruction-based* history. One queue holds the last 3 cycles (NOPs included) and another holds the last 3 **valid** ops (NOPs skipped). The constraints exclude ops based on the right queue, and `post_randomize` updates both. For resource overlap, model `[start, finish)` intervals and require any two ops on the same resource not to overlap.

| cycle | op | cycles hist (before) | valid_ops hist (before) | blocked |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | ADD | {} | {} | - |
| 1 | SUB | {ADD} | {ADD} | ADD |
| 2 | NOP | {ADD,SUB} | {ADD,SUB} | ADD, SUB |
| 3 | MUL | {ADD,SUB,NOP} | {ADD,SUB} | ADD, SUB |
| 4 | ADD | {SUB,NOP,MUL} | {ADD,SUB,MUL} | SUB |

### Solution
```systemverilog
typedef enum int {ADD, MUL, SUB, NOP} op_t;

class instruction_stream;
  rand op_t op;
  op_t cycles[$];       // last 3 cycles, including NOP
  op_t valid_ops[$];    // last 3 non-NOP instructions

  constraint c_add { foreach (cycles[i])    if (cycles[i] == ADD)    op != ADD; }
  constraint c_sub { foreach (valid_ops[i]) if (valid_ops[i] == SUB) op != SUB; }

  function void post_randomize();
    cycles.push_back(op);
    if (cycles.size() > 3) void'(cycles.pop_front());
    if (op != NOP) begin
      valid_ops.push_back(op);
      if (valid_ops.size() > 3) void'(valid_ops.pop_front());
    end
  endfunction
endclass

// Batch form: no two ops on the same resource overlap
class schedule;
  rand int unsigned start[10], finish[10], resource[10];
  constraint c_iv {
    foreach (start[i]) {
      start[i]  inside {[0:100]};
      finish[i] inside {[1:110]};
      finish[i] > start[i];
      resource[i] inside {[0:3]};
      foreach (start[j])
        if (i < j)
          (resource[i] != resource[j]) ||
          (finish[i] <= start[j]) || (finish[j] <= start[i]);   // half-open intervals
    }
  }
endclass
```

### Explanation
- The `if` inside `foreach` uses the **state** queue, so the constraint only applies to the current `op`.
- Half-open `[start, finish)` lets one op start exactly when another ends. Use `<` if a gap cycle is needed.
- The resource check is written as a disjunction, so the solver may pick different resources or non-overlapping times.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Clarify "within 3 cycles": does that include the current cycle? If so, keep 2 history entries.
- Update history on **issue/acceptance**, not on every `randomize()` call. A retry after a failed randomize must not shift the window.
- Whole-program form: `rand op_t prog[100]; foreach (prog[i]) foreach (prog[j]) if (j > i && j - i <= 3 && prog[i] == ADD) prog[j] != ADD;`.

### Follow-ups
- *Read-after-write hazards?* Randomize `dst`/`src` registers and require `src[j] != dst[i]` for `j - i < latency`.
- *Coverage?* Cover the distance between consecutive ADDs and between SUBs (bins 1, 2, 3, 4+).

hwinterview-046:
**Key idea:** Make the **position** of each row's maximum a random variable (`max_col[r]`). Every other element in that row must be strictly smaller, which gives a unique max. Then require the max values to differ across rows. Unique minima use the same pattern with `>`. Neighbor rules use guarded `r+1` and `c+1` indices.

| row | c0 | c1 | c2 | c3 | max_col | max |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 12 | **87** | 40 | 3 | 1 | 87 |
| 1 | **66** | 9 | 21 | 65 | 0 | 66 |
| 2 | 5 | 18 | 2 | **91** | 3 | 91 |

### Solution
```systemverilog
class row_maxima #(int R = 3, int C = 4);
  rand int m[R][C];
  rand int unsigned max_col[R];
  constraint c {
    foreach (max_col[r]) max_col[r] < C;
    foreach (m[r, c]) {
      m[r][c] inside {[0:99]};
      if (c != max_col[r]) m[r][c] < m[r][max_col[r]];   // strict: unique max
    }
    foreach (max_col[r]) foreach (max_col[s])
      if (r < s) m[r][max_col[r]] != m[s][max_col[s]];    // max values differ
  }
endclass

// Two 3x3 matrices, each with a unique minimum; minima differ
class matrix_minima;
  rand int a[9], b[9];
  rand int unsigned ia, ib;
  constraint c {
    ia < 9; ib < 9;
    foreach (a[i]) {
      a[i] inside {[0:99]}; b[i] inside {[0:99]};
      if (i != ia) a[i] > a[ia];
      if (i != ib) b[i] > b[ib];
    }
    a[ia] != b[ib];
  }
endclass

// Adjacent cells differ (each pair checked once)
class adj_diff #(int R = 4, int C = 4);
  rand bit [3:0] m[R][C];
  constraint c {
    foreach (m[r, c]) {
      if (c + 1 < C) m[r][c] != m[r][c+1];
      if (r + 1 < R) m[r][c] != m[r+1][c];
    }
  }
endclass
```

### Explanation
- Indexing an array with a random variable (`m[r][max_col[r]]`) is legal in constraints. The solver treats it as a multiplexer.
- "Different max **positions**" is not the same rule as "different max **values**". Read the prompt carefully.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- The domain must be big enough: R distinct maxima, each larger than C-1 other row values.
- "One maximum per sub-square": first decide whether blocks are disjoint tiles or sliding windows, then add one winner coordinate per block.

### Follow-ups
- *Column maxima too?* Add `max_row[c]` with the same pattern. Watch for conflicts (a saddle point).
- *Sorted rows?* `if (c > 0) m[r][c] > m[r][c-1];`, and the max is then always in the last column.

hwinterview-047:
**Key idea:** A clockwise 90-degree rotation maps `in[r][c] -> out[c][R-1-r]`, and an R x C input becomes C x R. Counter-clockwise: `in[r][c] -> out[C-1-c][r]`. Because the transform is fixed, you can randomize the input and dimensions, then compute the output procedurally, or tie both with equality constraints when they must be solved together.

**Worked example (R=2, C=3, clockwise):**
| in | c0 | c1 | c2 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| r0 | a | b | c |
| r1 | d | e | f |

| out | c0 | c1 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| r0 | d | a |
| r1 | e | b |
| r2 | f | c |

### Solution
```systemverilog
class rotate_matrix #(int MAX = 8);
  rand int unsigned rows, cols;
  rand int in_m[MAX][MAX];
  int out_m[MAX][MAX];

  constraint c_dim  { rows inside {[1:MAX]}; cols inside {[1:MAX]}; }
  constraint c_data {
    foreach (in_m[r, c])
      if (r < rows && c < cols) in_m[r][c] inside {[0:255]};
      else                      in_m[r][c] == 0;
  }

  function void post_randomize();          // deterministic transform
    foreach (out_m[r, c]) out_m[r][c] = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
      for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
        out_m[c][rows-1-r] = in_m[r][c];   // clockwise
  endfunction
endclass
```

Fully constraint-based form (both arrays `rand`):
```systemverilog
rand int out_c[MAX][MAX];
constraint c_rot {
  foreach (in_m[r, c])
    if (r < rows && c < cols) out_c[c][rows-1-r] == in_m[r][c];
  foreach (out_c[r, c])
    if (r >= cols || c >= rows) out_c[r][c] == 0;
}
```

### Explanation
- Fixed-size storage with random "active" dimensions avoids solving ragged dynamic arrays.
- Check: four clockwise rotations give back the original. Clockwise followed by counter-clockwise is the identity. Both are good self-checks.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Index `out_c[c][rows-1-r]` with random `rows`: the solver must handle a random index. Solve `rows`/`cols` first (sizes and dimensions should be solved before data).
- Square odd-size requirement: add `rows == cols; rows % 2 == 1;`.

### Follow-ups
- *In-place rotation of an N x N matrix?* Transpose, then reverse each row.
- *180 degrees?* `out[R-1-r][C-1-c] = in[r][c]`.

hwinterview-048:
**Key idea:** A board where each cell is just 0/1/2 can be **unreachable**. Legal (reachable) positions with X moving first need: `#X == #O` or `#X == #O + 1`, at most one winner, and the winner consistent with the move count (X wins means `#X = #O + 1`, O wins means `#X == #O`).

| X | O | X |
| --- | --- | --- |
| O | X | O |
| . | . | X |

Above: #X = 4, #O = 3, X wins on the diagonal, so the position is legal.

### Solution
```systemverilog
class tic_tac_toe;
  typedef enum bit [1:0] {EMPTY = 0, X = 1, O = 2} cell_e;
  rand cell_e board[9];
  rand int unsigned nx, no;
  rand bit xwin, owin;

  constraint c_count {
    nx == board.sum() with (int'(item == X));
    no == board.sum() with (int'(item == O));
    nx == no || nx == no + 1;
  }
  constraint c_win {
    xwin == ((board[0]==X && board[1]==X && board[2]==X) || (board[3]==X && board[4]==X && board[5]==X) ||
             (board[6]==X && board[7]==X && board[8]==X) || (board[0]==X && board[3]==X && board[6]==X) ||
             (board[1]==X && board[4]==X && board[7]==X) || (board[2]==X && board[5]==X && board[8]==X) ||
             (board[0]==X && board[4]==X && board[8]==X) || (board[2]==X && board[4]==X && board[6]==X));
    owin == ((board[0]==O && board[1]==O && board[2]==O) || (board[3]==O && board[4]==O && board[5]==O) ||
             (board[6]==O && board[7]==O && board[8]==O) || (board[0]==O && board[3]==O && board[6]==O) ||
             (board[1]==O && board[4]==O && board[7]==O) || (board[2]==O && board[5]==O && board[8]==O) ||
             (board[0]==O && board[4]==O && board[8]==O) || (board[2]==O && board[4]==O && board[6]==O));
    !(xwin && owin);
    xwin -> (nx == no + 1);
    owin -> (nx == no);
  }

  function void print();
    string s[3] = '{".", "X", "O"};
    for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++)
      $display("%s %s %s", s[board[3*r]], s[board[3*r+1]], s[board[3*r+2]]);
  endfunction
endclass
```
The 8 win lines are written out explicitly; solvers handle flat boolean terms more reliably than nested lookups into a line table.

### Explanation
- Count consistency enforces alternation with X starting.
- `xwin -> nx == no + 1`: X's winning move was the last move, so O can't have moved after it.
- One more subtle rule: X can win with two lines at once (for example a cross through the center on move 9). That is legal, so don't forbid multiple X lines.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Without the winner/count link, you get boards where O "won" and X then kept playing.
- A **game** generator is different. It randomizes a sequence of moves (pick an empty cell, alternate players, stop on win/draw). That fits better in a procedural sequence.

### Follow-ups
- *Generate only finished games?* Add `xwin || owin || (nx + no == 9)`.
- *Use as DUT stimulus?* Cover each of the 8 lines won by X and by O, plus draws.

hwinterview-049:
**Key idea:** A sync FIFO is checked against a queue model, with exact flags in its single clock domain. An async FIFO keeps **binary** pointers locally for RAM addressing, converts them to **Gray** (exactly one bit changes per increment), and synchronizes each Gray pointer into the other domain. Empty and full are computed against the *synchronized*, and therefore stale, opposite pointer. That makes them **pessimistic but safe**.

| bin (4b, depth 8 + wrap bit) | Gray | changed bit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0110 | 0101 | - |
| 0111 | 0100 | bit0 |
| 1000 | 1100 | bit3 |
| 1001 | 1101 | bit0 |

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "wclk",          "wave": "p........" },
  { "name": "wptr_gray",     "wave": "=.=.=.=..", "data": ["0101", "0100", "1100", "1101"] },
  { "name": "rclk",          "wave": "P...P...P" },
  { "name": "sync1 (rclk)",  "wave": "x...=...=", "data": ["0100", "1101"] },
  { "name": "rq2_wptr",      "wave": "x.......=", "data": ["0100"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "destination may skip Gray values (0100 -> 1101) but never sees a torn mix of bits" } }
```

### Solution
```systemverilog
// Write domain (depth 8, ASIZE = 3, pointers are ASIZE+1 bits)
assign wbin_next  = wbin + (winc & ~wfull);
assign wgray_next = (wbin_next >> 1) ^ wbin_next;
// full: next write Gray == synchronized read Gray with the top 2 bits inverted
assign wfull_val  = (wgray_next == {~wq2_rptr[ASIZE:ASIZE-1], wq2_rptr[ASIZE-2:0]});
always_ff @(posedge wclk or negedge wrst_n)
  if (!wrst_n) {wbin, wptr, wfull} <= '0;
  else         {wbin, wptr, wfull} <= {wbin_next, wgray_next, wfull_val};

// Read domain
assign rbin_next   = rbin + (rinc & ~rempty);
assign rgray_next  = (rbin_next >> 1) ^ rbin_next;
assign rempty_val  = (rgray_next == rq2_wptr);    // empty when equal
```

**Verification checklist**
```systemverilog
// Gray pointer changes at most one bit per source clock
a_gray_w: assert property (@(posedge wclk) disable iff (!wrst_n) $countones(wptr ^ $past(wptr)) <= 1);
a_gray_r: assert property (@(posedge rclk) disable iff (!rrst_n) $countones(rptr ^ $past(rptr)) <= 1);
// Pointers never advance while full / empty (no overflow / underflow)
a_no_ovf: assert property (@(posedge wclk) disable iff (!wrst_n) wfull  |=> $stable(wbin));
a_no_unf: assert property (@(posedge rclk) disable iff (!rrst_n) rempty |=> $stable(rbin));
```
In practice the end-to-end check is a queue scoreboard (push in the wclk monitor, pop and compare in the rclk monitor) plus randomized clock ratios and phases. Add CDC structural lint (2-FF synchronizers on Gray buses only) and an STA max-delay or skew constraint on the Gray bus.

### Explanation
- Gray coding means a sample taken mid-transition sees either the old value or the new one, never a corrupt mix. A **binary** pointer could be sampled as an arbitrary value.
- The destination can see the Gray pointer jump several steps between samples (fast writer, slow reader). That's fine. Don't assert "one bit change per destination clock" on the synchronized copy.
- Flags lag by 2 to 3 destination cycles. The FIFO reports full or empty a little early and never a little late.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- **Non-power-of-two depth:** a binary counter that wraps at, say, 6 does not give a 1-bit Gray change at the wrap (`101 -> 000`). Options: a power-of-two ring with a logical capacity limit, or a reflected Gray sequence centered in the code space (for even depths).
- Bus skew on the Gray bits larger than one source period can break the single-bit guarantee. Constrain it with `set_max_delay -datapath_only`.
- Test reset of one domain while the other is active, stopped clocks, and 1:1 phase-shifted clocks.

### Follow-ups
- *Why compare next pointers?* It registers the flags without an extra cycle of lag.
- *Almost-full?* Convert the synchronized Gray pointer back to binary and subtract.

hwinterview-052:
**Key idea:** Both arbiters share the basic checks: grant is one-hot or zero, grant implies a request, a request leads to some grant, correct reset state, and the grant holds during a stall. The **priority model** is what differs. Fixed priority: the lowest index wins. Round-robin: search starting from the pointer, which moves past the winner after each service. Test fixed priority for correct precedence (starvation is allowed). Test round-robin for **bounded wait**.

| req[3:0] | fixed winner | RR (ptr=2) winner | RR ptr after |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1111 | 0 | 2 | 3 |
| 1011 | 0 | 3 | 0 |
| 0110 | 1 | 2 | 3 |
| 1000 | 3 | 3 | 0 |

### Explanation
```systemverilog
// Reference models
function automatic int fixed_pred(bit [3:0] req);
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) if (req[i]) return i;
  return -1;
endfunction

function automatic int rr_pred(bit [3:0] req, int ptr);
  for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) if (req[(ptr + k) % 4]) return (ptr + k) % 4;
  return -1;
endfunction

// Common assertions
a_1hot : assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) $onehot0(gnt));
a_req  : assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) (gnt & ~req) == 0);
a_live : assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) (|req) |-> (|gnt));
// RR fairness (1-cycle service): requester i waits at most N-1 grants
genvar i;
for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) begin : g_fair
  a_fair: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
            req[i] |-> ##[0:3] (gnt[i] || !req[i]));
end
```
The fairness property assumes 1-cycle service and lets a requester withdraw its request before it is served.

**Stimulus and coverage**
| Item | Fixed | Round-robin |
| --- | --- | --- |
| All 2^N masks | yes | yes, x every pointer value |
| Persistent full contention | low index starves (expected) | each served once per N grants |
| Intermittent high-priority | low wins only in gaps | rotation continues |
| Pointer wrap | n/a | ptr N-1 -> 0 |
| Stall during grant | grant held | grant held, ptr not advanced until accepted |
| Reset mid-service | grant 0 | ptr reset value |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- The pointer must advance on **accepted service**, not just on grant, if grants can be stalled.
- Some RR designs set `ptr = ptr + 1` instead of `winner + 1`. That is less fair, so the model must match the spec.
- A cycle-count fairness bound is only valid if service time is bounded. Otherwise count grants.

### Follow-ups
- *Formal?* Fairness is a liveness-style property and works well with bounded-proof formal on small N.
- *Masked RR implementation?* Two fixed-priority arbiters (masked and unmasked requests), using the masked result if any.
